  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Increasing chances for showers and thunderstorms \nearly this morning and throughout the day today.\n\nA weak mid-level shortwave trough with warm frontal segment will \npush across the region this morning. As such, expect showers to \nincrease in coverage ahead of these features from west to east this \nmorning. A secondary round of showers with embedded thunderstorms \nwill develop this afternoon and evening with a cold frontal passage. \nInstability will initially be muted earlier in the day from the \nfirst round of showers and cloud cover, however with some break in \ncloud cover in the early afternoon should build weak to moderate \ninstability. Additionally marginal shear profiles with 0-6 km bulk \nshear of 30 to 40 knots could develop an isolated strong to\nPrimary hazards are damaging winds and large hail. The greatest risk\nfor strong to severe storms will lie across western New York. An\nisolated tornado can't be ruled out across the western Southern\nTier. \n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Trending warmer this weekend and into next \nweek.\n\nA mid to upper level ridge will begin to build across the Great \nLakes this weekend before shifting east into the Northeastern \nportions of the CONUS through next week. As such, temperatures will \ngradually increase by the end of the weekend and into early next \nweek. Heat and humidity will then increase further through the \nmiddle of next week, which may result in the potential for heat-\nrelated headlines as the risk for dangerous heat indices increase.\n\nThis all being said, heat will greatly depend on the placement of \nthe ridge axis. If the axis lies to the west of the area, there is a \nthreat for \"ridge runners\" next week which not only would decrease \nthe chance for heat headlines but also introduce daily chances for \nrain.",
